A thorough investigation of the WLAN segment and VRRP/keepalived subsystem finds no
fault. The VLAN 40 (10.6.0.0/24) WiFi gateway VIP 10.6.0.1 is correctly held by
dswa2 as the VRRP MASTER (priority 105, instance VI_40) and is reachable with zero
packet loss from laptop1. dswa1 is correctly in the BACKUP role (priority 1) and
does not hold the VIP. Both keepalived processes are running and the VRRP state is
fully consistent with the keepalived.conf on both distribution switches.

The access-layer VLAN plumbing on aswa1 is correct: eth1 toward laptop1 is an access
port for VLAN 40 (PVID 40, egress-untagged), and the trunk to dswa1 (eth2) carries
VLAN 40 in forwarding state. The STP-blocked state on aswa1.eth3 toward dswa2 is
expected behaviour that prevents a bridging loop in the access-distribution square
topology; traffic reaches dswa2 via the dswa1→dswa2 LACP bond peer link without
interruption.

OSPF on both dswa1 and dswa2 is fully converged with three neighbours each and
correct default routes toward csw1 and csw2. The 10.6.0.0/24 prefix is advertised
into OSPF by dswa2 and reachable from all directions. From laptop1, pings to the
DNS server (10.5.0.4), internal hosts (10.1.0.100, 10.3.0.100), and a WAN-facing
traceroute all succeed without loss.

Root cause: There is no fault. The user's report of "lost network" does not reflect
the actual lab state; the WLAN segment (VLAN 40) and its keepalived VRRP gateway are
fully operational.

Corrective action: No intervention is required. If the user's device still reports a
problem, confirm the physical/wireless association and verify the client-side IP
configuration — the infrastructure itself is healthy.
